<p>A proposed law to allow assisted dying for some terminally ill adults in England and Wales failed, or appeared set to fail, after parliamentary time ran out, despite earlier backing in the House of Commons. The Terminally Ill Adults (End of Life) Bill would have allowed adults judged to have fewer than six months to live to apply for an assisted death, subject to approval by two doctors and an expert panel. Supporters said the measure would give dying patients more choice, but critics in Parliament proposed extensive amendments and delays that kept it from clearing the House of Lords. For now, the law in England and Wales remains unchanged. But advocates say they will try again, keeping the issue alive in British public debate. The case has also renewed scrutiny of the House of Lords, with the New York Times reporting that seven unelected peers proposed hundreds of amendments that helped block the bill’s progress.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/47pSVqSUUYZhqeby1iZnJQFI5tSj9o5BjJ2xpXzaewvHAu3Paon8VHvP9LLIIoA0vnuMiGapAPwVL_uIeLSzsMl1nvPkBY0yfMsNjGI4geZRp6_q5V4tz4Ve2SpQmFpim3rwcCWPZDzDckfG4c89BmhAjnONELgcUWGTz9UzMGbn6H2UgDfudeSs00KSFI6LtK6mFuo8fgnlfa7zi-Av-PeFXnD_2rKj' alt='Members of the House of Lords and House of Commons during the State Opening of Parliament in the House of Lords in London in 2023.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Lords tactics: A small group of peers proposed hundreds of amendments, helping use up the parliamentary time available for the measure.</li><li>Eligibility rules: Under the proposal, only terminally ill adults in England and Wales with fewer than six months to live could have sought an assisted death.</li><li>Advocates persist: After the bill failed, backers of legalisation said they would try again to change the law.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Bill supporters: Supporters argued the legislation would legalise assisted dying for terminally ill people and said, after the measure failed, that they would try again. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/24/uk-bill-allowing-terminally-ill-to-end-their-own-lives-obstructed'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>House of Lords critics: Critics of the Lords’ role said seven unelected peers were able to hinder the bill by tabling hundreds of amendments, raising questions about the chamber’s influence over legislation approved by elected MPs. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/24/world/europe/uk-assisted-dying-bill-lords.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Opponents and revising peers: Peers in the House of Lords effectively talked out the bill through amendments and delay, showing continued resistance to changing the law on assisted dying. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/europe/article/3351381/uk-bill-allow-assisted-dying-fails-parliamentary-time-runs-out'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/bill-to-allow-assisted-dying-in-england-and-wales-set-to-fall-as-parliamentary-time-runs-out/article70901806.ece'>Bill to allow assisted dying in England and Wales set to fall as parliamentary time runs out</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/24/uk-bill-allowing-terminally-ill-to-end-their-own-lives-obstructed'>UK assisted dying bill fails after delays but advocates vow to try again</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/europe/article/3351381/uk-bill-allow-assisted-dying-fails-parliamentary-time-runs-out'>UK bill to allow assisted dying fails as parliamentary time runs out</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/24/world/europe/uk-assisted-dying-bill-lords.html'>In Britain, 7 Unelected Lords Help Block an Assisted Dying Bill</a> - nytimes.com</li></ul>